Why: A Windows accessibility call that hangs in one app no longer blocks calls into other apps: each blocking UI Automation call is admitted per target process (desktop-wide scans and hit-tests share one desktop gate), within a budget of four workers in all, and a stuck worker keeps its app's gate and its budget slot until its provider call really returns. Before, one process-wide gate meant a File Explorer Close that hung in its provider made a Calculator click 25 s later fail as busy. A click that times out shows again only the dialog owners it hid itself, not those of another app's click still running, and a call refused while its app is busy says that an earlier call to this app is still running.

